{{also|Unto}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|unto}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*untō}}, {{m|ang|*und tō}}, equivalent to {{prefix|en|un|t1=against; toward; up to|to}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|ont ti}} {{gloss|"until"; > {{cog|stq|antou|t=until}}}} (cf. {{cog|ofs|und|t=up to; till}}, {{cog|ofs|til|t=till; to}}), {{cog|osx|untō}}, {{m|osx|untuo|t=until}}, {{cog|goh|unze}}, {{m|goh|unzi}}, {{m|goh|unza|t=until}}, {{cog|non|und|t=as far as; up to}}, {{cog|got|𐌿𐌽𐍄𐌴|t=until; as long as}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʌntuː/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-unto.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Conjunction===
{{en-con}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|poetic}} Up to the [[time]] or [[degree]] that; [[until]]; [[till]].

===Preposition===
{{en-prep}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} Up to, indicating a motion towards a thing and then stopping at it.
#: ''Sir Gawain rode '''unto''' the nearby castle.''
# {{lb|en|archaic|or|poetic}} [[to|To]], indicating an indirect object
#: ''And the Lord said '''unto''' Moses {{...}}''
#* Sir Thomas Browne
#*: Again, whereas men affirm they perceive an addition of ponderosity in dead bodies, comparing them usually '''unto''' blocks and stones, whensoever they lift or carry them; this accessional preponderancy is rather in appearance than reality.

====Translations====
{{trans-top|Up to}}
* Arabic: {{t+|ar|إِلَى}}
* French: {{t+|fr|jusqu'à}}
* German: {{t+|de|zu}}
{{trans-mid}}
* Latin: {{t+|la|ad}}, {{t+|la|usque}}, {{t+|la|sub}}
* Turkish: -e {{t+|tr|doğru}}, -e {{t+|tr|kadar}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===See also===
